<header class="sg-head"><p class="featured-region">Protest and freedom · 1780 · Europe</p><h1 class="sg-title">Die Gedanken sind frei</h1><p class="sg-kid" data-say>An old German song that says no one can lock up your thoughts. People sang it against censorship and against the Nazis.</p></header>
<dl class="sg-facts"><div><dt>Words</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Music</dt><dd>traditional</dd></div><div><dt>Culture</dt><dd>German-speaking lands; later German student fraternities and the resistance to Nazism</dd></div><div><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd></div><div><dt>Place</dt><dd>German-speaking lands (Germany, Switzerland)</dd></div><div><dt>Date</dt><dd>first printed on leaflets about 1780</dd></div><div><dt>Type</dt><dd>Protest and freedom</dd></div><div><dt>The words</dt><dd>Public domain (traditional (leaflets of about 1780; best-known version by Hoffmann von Fallersleben, 1842))</dd></div></dl>
<div class="sg-body">
<section class="sg-sec" id="story"><h2>The story</h2><p>&quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/die-gedanken-sind-frei">Die Gedanken sind frei</a>&quot; (&quot;Thoughts Are Free&quot;) is a traditional German song about freedom of thought. The idea in its title is old: it was voiced in antiquity and again in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a> by the poets <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/walther-von-der-vogelweide">Walther von der Vogelweide</a>, Dietmar von Aist and Freidank. The song itself first appeared on leaflets around 1780, with four verses to which a fifth was later added. An early version, a dialogue between a prisoner in a tower and his beloved, was printed as &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/song-dynasty">Song</a> of the Persecuted in the Tower, after Swiss songs&quot; in Achim von Arnim and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/clemens-brentano">Clemens Brentano</a>'s folk-poetry collection Des Knaben Wunderhorn, about 1805. The words and the tune appear together in Lieder der Brienzer Mädchen, printed in Bern between 1810 and 1820. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hoffmann-von-fallersleben">Hoffmann von Fallersleben</a> took it into his collection of Silesian folk songs in 1842, as a song from Neukirch bei Schönau, and his version became the most popular.</p><p>From the time of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carlsbad-decrees">Carlsbad Decrees</a> and the age of Metternich, it became a popular protest song against political repression and censorship, above all among the banned student fraternities, the Burschenschaften. After the failed German revolution of 1848 the song was banned. In 1898 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/gustav-mahler">Gustav Mahler</a> set the Wunderhorn text for voice and orchestra.</p><p>Under the Nazi <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dictatorship">dictatorship</a> it mattered to parts of the German resistance. In 1935 the guards at the Lichtenburg <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/concentration-camp">concentration camp</a> ordered prisoners to put on a performance for Hitler's 46th birthday, and the imprisoned lawyer Hans Litten recited &quot;<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/die-gedanken-sind-frei">Die Gedanken sind frei</a>&quot; in answer. In 1942 Sophie Scholl of the White Rose resistance group played it on her flute outside the walls of Ulm prison, where her father Robert was held for calling Hitler a &quot;scourge of God&quot;. The American singer <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pete-seeger">Pete Seeger</a> recorded an adaptation in 1966.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="meaning"><h2>What it says</h2><p>A song of inner freedom: no prison or ruler can reach what a person thinks. Its mood is calm and quietly defiant.</p></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="words"><h2>The words</h2><div class="sg-show" role="group" aria-label="Show these columns"><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="orig">German</button><button type="button" aria-pressed="true" data-col="en">In English</button></div><div class="sg-blocks"><div class="sg-lyrics sg-cols-2"><div class="sg-colhead" aria-hidden="true"><span class="sg-o">German</span><span class="sg-e">In English</span></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Gedanken sind frei,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thoughts are free,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wer kann sie errathen?</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Who can guess them?</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sie fliegen vorbei</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>They fly past</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Wie nächtliche Schatten.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Like shadows of the night.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Kein Mensch kann sie wissen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No person can know them,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Kein Jäger sie schießen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No hunter shoot them.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Es bleibet dabei:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So it remains:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Gedanken sind frei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thoughts are free.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ich denke was ich will</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I think what I will</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und was mich beglücket,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And what makes me happy,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Doch alles in der Still</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>But all in quiet</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und wie es sich schicket.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And as is fitting.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mein Wunsch und Begehren</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My wish and desire</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Kann niemand verwehren.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>No one can forbid.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Es bleibet dabei:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>So it remains:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Gedanken sind frei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thoughts are free.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Sperrt man mich gleich ein</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Even if they lock me up</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Im finsteren Kerker,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>In a dark dungeon,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">So sind es doch nur</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Those are still only</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Vergebliche Werke;</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Futile works;</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Denn meine Gedanken</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>For my thoughts</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Zerreißen die Schranken</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Tear the barriers</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und Mauern entzwei:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And walls in two:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Gedanken sind frei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thoughts are free.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Jetzt will ich auf immer</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Now I will for ever</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Der Liebe entsagen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Renounce love</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und will mich nicht mehr</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And will no longer</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mit Grillen so plagen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Torment myself so with fancies.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Man kann ja im Herzen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>One can, after all, in one's heart</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Stets lachen und scherzen</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Always laugh and joke</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Und denken dabei:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>And think meanwhile:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Gedanken sind frei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thoughts are free.</span></p></div><div class="sg-st"><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ich liebe den Wein,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I love wine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mein Mädchen vor allen,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My girl above all,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die thut mir allein</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>She alone</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Am besten gefallen.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Pleases me best.</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Ich bin nicht alleine</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>I am not alone</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Bei meinem Glas Weine,</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>With my glass of wine,</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Mein Mädchen dabei:</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>My girl beside me:</span></p><p class="sg-ln"><span class="sg-o" lang="de">Die Gedanken sind frei.</span><span class="sg-e" data-say>Thoughts are free.</span></p></div></div></div><div class="sg-credit"><p>Original: Traditional; the text of Hoffmann von Fallersleben's Schlesische Volkslieder (1842). Text from <a href="https://de.wikisource.org/wiki/Die_Gedanken_sind_frei" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikisource</a>; public domain.</p><p>The English is the Institute&#8217;s own translation, made from the original for this page.</p></div><details class="sg-notes"><summary>Notes on the translation (1)</summary><ul><li>line 4.4: 'Grillen' are whims or gloomy fancies (literally 'crickets').</li></ul></details></section>
</div>
