<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired"><h2>What it inspired</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Song of the Persecuted in the Tower, in Des Knaben Wunderhorn (Achim von Arnim and Clemens Brentano)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1805</span><span class="sg-chain-h">early version in dialogue</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Schlesische Volkslieder mit Melodien (Hoffmann von Fallersleben)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1842</span><span class="sg-chain-h">most popular version</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Des Knaben Wunderhorn (Gustav Mahler)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1898</span><span class="sg-chain-h">new setting for voice and orchestra</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Dangerous Songs!? (Pete Seeger)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">1966</span><span class="sg-chain-h">adaptation</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">1899 (Netflix series)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">2022</span><span class="sg-chain-h">used in</span></li></ul></section>
<section class="sg-sec" id="inspired-by"><h2>What it drew on</h2><ul class="sg-chain"><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Walther von der Vogelweide, verse on the freedom of thought</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1200</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier expression of the idea</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Gedanke die sint ledic vrî (Dietmar von Aist)</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">12th century</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier expression of the idea</span></li><li><span class="sg-chain-n">Freidank, verse on the freedom of thought</span> <span class="sg-chain-y">c. 1229</span><span class="sg-chain-h">earlier expression of the idea</span></li></ul></section>
